From 9f1c4e7a2b6d8035c1a9e4f7b02d6c8a5e3f1907 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 15:20:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: cpu opp tables + cpu-supply (M9)

Give both CPU clusters an operating-points-v2 table and wire the two PMIC
rails that feed them, so cpufreq-dt can scale frequency AND voltage
instead of leaving both cluster PLLs on boot0's 1014 MHz forever.

  cluster0 (6x Cortex-A55, cpu0..cpu5) -> DCDC5 = VDD-CPUL (+ VDD-DSU)
  cluster1 (2x Cortex-A76, cpu6..cpu7) -> DCDC3 = VDD-CPUB

This is the patch of the series that can damage the board, so the
reasoning is written out in full rather than summarised.

WHY A DT WINDOW IS A WRITE, AND WHY IT IS ALLOWED HERE
======================================================

`regulator-min-microvolt` plus `regulator-max-microvolt` is not a
statement about the hardware. Both present => `constraints->apply_uV`
(of_regulator.c:99-111), and `machine_constraints_voltage()`
(core.c:1202-1262) then drives the rail into that window at probe,
logging "Bringing NuV into A-BuV". A ceiling without a floor is not a
weaker form of the same thing, it is rejected outright with -EINVAL
(core.c:1291). There is no describe-only shape.

This project already paid for that lesson. An earlier PMIC-constraints patch gave 16 rails
datasheet windows; on the 8 GiB board, 2026-07-21 18:44, the kernel pulled ELDO1 =
the LPDDR5 die rail VDD2L from 1.1 V down to 0.95 V at t=1.79 s against
DRAM that boot0 had trained and that was in use, and late cleanup
switched two more rails off at t=31.8 s. That patch was dropped, not fixed,
and the conclusion was: a window may
come back only together with the consumer that owns the rail, where the
write is the whole point.

That is this patch, and it is the only rail pair that qualifies today:
the OPP core is the owner of VDD-CPUL/VDD-CPUB, changing them is its job,
and every value it may write is enumerated below.

Two consequences of the same mechanism are used deliberately here:

 - the floor is 800000 on both rails, which is exactly what both rails
   measure at boot, so `current_uV` is inside the window and probe writes
   nothing. The log line from that incident must not appear for either rail.

 - `regulator-always-on` is not decoration. Without it the node carries
   REGULATOR_CHANGE_STATUS (of_regulator.c:133) and
   `regulator_late_cleanup()` (core.c:6492-6534) disables any rail whose
   `use_count` is still 0 roughly 30 s after boot. The OPP core does take
   a reference (`regulator_enable()` in `_opp_config_regulator_single()`,
   opp/core.c:1140), but only on the first OPP transition. If cpufreq-dt
   does not probe -- unreadable speed bin, missing CPU clock, driver not
   built -- nothing holds the reference and the kernel would switch off
   the rail that the CPU running it is powered from. Both rails are
   measured on (reg 0x10 = 0xff), so the enable itself is a no-op.

WHAT ACTUALLY BREAKS, IN BOTH DIRECTIONS
========================================

A CMOS gate switches by charging its load through a channel whose current
rises with the gate overdrive, so a higher rail voltage means a shorter
propagation delay; heat lowers carrier mobility and lengthens it again;
and a die that came out of the line better reaches the same delay at a
lower voltage, which is what the speed bin records. The PMIC turns a
7-bit register code into millivolts on the rail (500 mV + n * 10 mV), and
the OPP table is the contract that says which frequency is allowed to
demand which code.

Too little voltage for the requested frequency: the critical path no
longer completes inside one clock period, a flip-flop latches a value
that is still settling, and the machine takes a wrong branch, an
exception at a nonsense address, or corrupts a memory write. It does not
harm the silicon and it is not reliably reproducible -- it appears under
load, at temperature, on one chip out of a batch. The dangerous property
is that it looks like a software bug.

Too much voltage: dynamic power goes with V^2 and leakage worse than
linearly, so the die heats, which is merely inefficient; the real cost is
the oxide field. Time-dependent dielectric breakdown, hot-carrier
injection and NBTI all accelerate steeply with voltage, and they are
cumulative and invisible -- the part keeps working until it does not,
months later, with no error counter to read. Above the datasheet absolute
maximum (VDD-CPUB / VDD-CPUL: 1.2 V, DS Tab. 5-1) the damage is immediate
rather than gradual.

Hence the asymmetry that shapes this patch: an unidentified chip gets the
HIGHEST voltage any characterised bin needs, never the average and never
an interpolation. Guessing high costs efficiency, guessing low costs
stability, and both are recoverable; only exceeding the datasheet is not.
No OPP here goes beyond 1050 mV, which is 150 mV under the absolute
maximum and is the highest value that occurs in any of the nine vendor
bins.

WHERE EVERY NUMBER COMES FROM
=============================

Frequencies (spec + silicon, both boards identical). f = 26 MHz * N with
N read from CPU_L/B_PLL_CTRL_REG bits[15:8] (N is raw, no +1; UM field
table). 416 MHz is the only step using EXT_DIVP = /2, because 26*16 would
put the VCO under the documented 480 MHz floor:

  MHz  416  780 1014 1196 1404 1508 1612 1716 1794 1898 1950 2002
  N     32   30   39   46   54   58   62   66   69   73   75   77

The 26 MHz reference is silicon, not the 24 MHz our DT modelled before
`arm64-dts-a733-model-the-26mhz-dcxo`: XO_CTRL0 @0x07090160 reads 0x983FB0F7 byte-identical on the 6 GiB board and
the 8 GiB board, DCXO_ST[15:14] = 0b10 = 26 MHz.

Voltages, MEASURED (PMIC register readback taken while the PLL register
showed the matching N):

  bin 0x05: the 8 GiB board, the one pristine vendor image. Fully trustworthy.
  bin 0x04: the 6 GiB board, running OUR OWN rebuild, not a vendor image. It is a
            real measurement of what our build drives on bin-0x04
            silicon; it is not an independent confirmation of the vendor
            table, because our build carries that table.

Both columns match the vendor vf tables step for step. That is recorded
here as an observation made AFTER the registers were read, not as the
reason to trust them: the vendor tables were a hint until the PMIC
confirmed them, and they stay a hint everywhere the PMIC did not.

  MHz   | A55 0x04 | A55 0x05 | A76 0x04 | A76 0x05
  ------+----------+----------+----------+---------
   416  |   800    |   800    |   800    |   800
   780  |   800    |   800    |   800    |   800
  1014  |   800    |   800    |   800    |   800
  1196  |   800    |   800    |   800    |   800
  1404  |   840    |   800    |   800    |   800
  1508  |   880    |   820    |   820    |   800
  1612  |   920    |   860    |   840    |   820
  1716  |   960    |   900    |   870    |   850
  1794  |  1000    |   950    |   920    |   900
  1898  |    -     |    -     |   970    |   940
  1950  |    -     |    -     |  1000    |   970
  2002  |    -     |    -     |  1050    |  1000

Voltages, CARRIED OVER AND NOT SILICON-VERIFIED: the entire worst-case
column, i.e. every unnamed `opp-microvolt` above 800000. Those come from
the seven vendor bins we own no chip of. Nobody has read a PMIC register
on a 0x00/0x01/0x02/0x03/0x06/0x11/0x12/0x14/0x15 part, here or anywhere
else in this project.

Coding check for the values that end up on the wire (from the AXP323 V1.1 datasheet,
and matched against silicon):

  800 mV -> n = (800-500)/10 = 30 = 0x1e, +DVM bit7 -> 0x9e
  1000 mV -> n = 50 = 0x32 -> 0xb2
  1050 mV -> n = 55 = 0x37 -> 0xb7

0x9e is exactly what registers 0x14 and 0x16 read on the 6 GiB board on 2026-07-22
(i2cget -f -y 1 0x36) and what a register readout on the pristine vendor image shows for the 8 GiB board. Every
voltage in this patch is a multiple of 10 mV inside 500..1200 mV, so each
one is an exact PMIC code and nothing is rounded on the way down.

SPEED BIN: THE CONTRACT WITH PATCH `nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse`
=======================================

`nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse` has landed; this table is written against its code, not against an
assumption. Three columns per operating point:

    opp-microvolt-speed4   bin 0x04, measured
    opp-microvolt-speed5   bin 0x05, measured
    opp-microvolt          worst case, unnamed

  opp-supported-hw = <0x31> = BIT(0) | BIT(4) | BIT(5)

The bit position IS the raw fuse value: `nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse` takes bits[15:8] of the
32-bit word at SID fuse offset 0x4c (ARM 0x0300624c) and uses it directly
as the speed index, `supported_hw = BIT(bin)`, `prop_name = "speed<bin>"`.
So bin 0x04 -> BIT(4) -> `opp-microvolt-speed4`. No mapping table exists
anywhere; DT alone decides which bins are described.

Bit 0 is the worst-case column and it is not a bin. `nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse` returns index 0
deterministically for every die it cannot act on: fuse bits[15:8] read 0
(an unprogrammed fuse and a part graded 0 are indistinguishable from
outside), bin >= 32, the second fuse copy in bits[23:16] is programmed
(its meaning is unverified and all three of our dies read zero there), or
no OPP in DT claims that bin. Index 0 then means `prop_name = "speed0"`,
no `opp-microvolt-speed0` property exists, and `_parse_named_prop()`
(opp/of.c:551-573) falls back to the unnamed `opp-microvolt`. That
fallback is also why every operating point carries the unnamed property:
a column forgotten in a future edit degrades to the worst case, never to
a wrong voltage.

Every operating point carries BIT(0), so the worst-case path yields the
complete ladder rather than a crippled machine. And both cluster tables
declare the same bin set {0, 4, 5}: `nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse`'s `sunxi_cpufreq_dt_speed_mask()`
ORs the masks within a CPU and ANDs across CPUs, so a bin described on
only one cluster would demote the whole machine to the worst case. Any
edit that adds a bin has to add it to both tables.

The eFuse itself is silicon-proven: 0x0300624c reads 0x00000598 on the 8 GiB board,
0x00000496 on the 6 GiB board, 0x00000596 on a second 6 GiB board -> bins 0x05 / 0x04 / 0x05, three
chips, read out of the fuse block on each.

If `nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse` is absent or fails, `_opp_is_supported()` (opp/of.c:497-548)
disables every OPP carrying `opp-supported-hw`, cpufreq-dt finds an empty
table and refuses to probe: boot frequency, no DVFS, no rail movement.
Safe, and visible in the log.

The table node also carries `compatible =
"allwinner,sun60i-a733-operating-points"` (`nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse` matches on it in
`cpu_opp_match_list` and added it to the binding) and `nvmem-cells =
<&cpu_speed_grade>`, the cell `nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse`'s own `sid: efuse@3006000` node
defines. No second SID node is added here.

THE WORST-CASE COLUMN
=====================

Per frequency, the maximum over all nine vendor bins, made monotonic by
running maximum:

  A55: 416/780/1014 @800, 1196 @850, 1404 @950, 1508 @1000,
       1612/1716/1794 @1050
  A76: 416/780/1014/1196 @800, 1404 @850, 1508 @900, 1612 @950,
       1716 @1000, 1794/1898/1950/2002 @1050

Running maximum rather than raw maximum because the raw one is not
monotonic (A55 1612 needs 1050 in bin 0x01 while 1716 needs 1000 in bin
0x02, the only bin that has it), and a table where a higher frequency
asks for less voltage is a table that undervolts something.

780 MHz needs one note: bin 0x00 has no 780 MHz entry, it uses 728 MHz.
Its neighbours in that column, 728 and 1014, are both 800 mV, so 780 MHz
at 800 mV is bracketed on both sides rather than extrapolated. Same for
the A76 1612 MHz entry: bin 0x00 skips it, but its 1586 MHz point is
850 mV and its 1690 MHz point is 900 mV, both under the 950 mV shipped
here.

KNOWN RESIDUAL RISK, deliberately not mitigated in this patch. The
worst-case column covers voltage completely and frequency only partly.
Bins 0x03 and 0x06 stop at 1404 MHz (A55) and 1612 MHz (A76) in the
vendor table, so an unidentified die that is in fact a 0x03 would be
offered 1794/2002 MHz -- at the highest voltage any bin uses, but at a
frequency its own bin never validated. Capping the worst-case ladder
there was written and dropped: `nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse` requires every OPP to be reachable
through BIT(0), and splitting that decision across two patches is worse
than the risk. The honest fix is a measured column for such a die if one
ever turns up, not an interpolation. Recorded here so the next person
finds it named rather than discovers it.

THE REGULATOR WINDOWS, WITH THE ARITHMETIC
==========================================

Not copied from a ceiling-only list of datasheet limits: such a list has no
floor precisely because it must never become a DT node unchanged, and
a ceiling-only node does not even register (-EINVAL).

Derived instead as: floor = the measured boot voltage, ceiling = the
highest voltage any OPP in this patch can request.

  DCDC5 / VDD-CPUL + VDD-DSU:
    floor   800000  = boot value, the 6 GiB board reg 0x16 = 0x9e, the 8 GiB board identical
    ceiling 1050000 = max over {A55 bin 0x04 top 1000, bin 0x05 top 950,
                                worst case 1050 at 1612..1794}
  DCDC3 / VDD-CPUB:
    floor   800000  = boot value, the 6 GiB board reg 0x14 = 0x9e, the 8 GiB board identical
    ceiling 1050000 = max over {A76 bin 0x04 top 1050, bin 0x05 top 1000,
                                worst case 1050 at 1794..2002}

1050 mV is 150 mV (12.5 %) under the 1.2 V DS Tab. 5-1 absolute maximum
for both pins, and it is the largest value that appears in any of the
nine vendor bins for either cluster. It is 50 mV above the 0.8..1.0 V
range the schematic annotates; that annotation is a nominal operating
range, not a rating, and DS Tab. 5-1 is the rating.

One asymmetry worth flagging rather than hiding: on DCDC3 the 1050 mV
ceiling is a value we have measured (bin 0x04 A76 at 2002 MHz on the 6 GiB board).
On DCDC5 it is not -- our A55 measurements top out at 1000 mV -- and it
becomes reachable only through the worst-case column, i.e. only on a die
we cannot identify. It is a vendor-table number inside the datasheet
limit, and the alternative (an 1000000 ceiling) would silently drop the
worst-case entries at 1612..1794 MHz via
`_opp_supported_by_regulators()`, leaving an unidentified die with a
1508 MHz cap and no explanation in the log. Bounded at 1050 mV is the
better of the two.

Both endpoints are exact PMIC codes (n = 30 and n = 55), so the clamp
loop at the end of `machine_constraints_voltage()` narrows nothing.

The window is also a second gate on the table itself:
`_opp_supported_by_regulators()` (opp/core.c:1930) marks any OPP outside
the constraints unavailable at parse time rather than driving the rail
out of the window. If a future edit adds an entry above the ceiling, it
is dropped with a warning instead of applied.

min != max, so REGULATOR_CHANGE_VOLTAGE is set (of_regulator.c:108) and
DVFS is permitted at all. A single-value window would have made the rail
read-only.

Expect two "supply vin23 not found, using dummy regulator" /
"supply vin45 not found" warnings (core.c:2235): the AXP318W descriptors
name an input supply for the DCDCs and we do not model the PMIC input
rails. Harmless, and giving them nodes would mean more rail nodes, and a
rail gets a DT node only together with its consumer.

RAMP DELAY, AND WHAT clock-latency-ns CAN HONESTLY SAY
======================================================

`regulator-ramp-delay = <1280>` (uV/us) = 10 mV per 7.8125 us. The step
size is the DCDC2..5 coding (10 mV below 1.2 V) and the 7.8125 us is the
default slew rate of REG 0x1B bit5, read as 0 on the 6 GiB board on 2026-07-22
(0x1b = 0x00). DVM is enabled on both rails (bit7 of 0x14/0x16), so the
PMIC really does walk the codes instead of stepping.

This is not a nicety. `_regulator_set_voltage_time()` (core.c:3653)
returns 0 when neither the constraint nor the descriptor carries a ramp
delay -- and axp20x_ops_range sets no `.ramp_delay` -- so
`_regulator_do_set_voltage()` would `fsleep(0)` after commanding the new
code. The i2c write returns as soon as the register is written, not when
the rail arrives. The OPP core raises voltage BEFORE frequency when
scaling up (`_set_opp()`, opp/core.c:1326-1360), and without the delay
that ordering guarantees nothing: the PLL would go to 2002 MHz up to
195 us before the rail reaches 1050 mV. That is the undervolt failure
mode above, built in by omission.

The property never programs the chip: `set_machine_constraints()` calls
`ops->set_ramp_delay` only if the op exists (core.c:1506), and
axp20x_ops_range has none.

`clock-latency-ns` is deliberately NOT set, although this binding allows
it. The vendor writes 244144 on every entry; 244144 ns is 1/4096 s, a
constant that appears verbatim in DTs for unrelated SoCs. It is not a
measurement of this PLL and we cannot turn it into one. The A733 UM
documents PLL_UPDATE, LOCK and the glitch-free mux, and its Table 4-2
gives a lock time of 1.5 ms for CPU_L_PLL, CPU_B_PLL and CPU_DSU_PLL
(UM V1.00 p. 20 / PDF p. 41; same value in V0.92 p. 20 / PDF p. 40; the
column `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-ve-plls-module-clocks-gates-and-resets` cites for the VE PLLs). That is a specification bound, not a
measured relock time for an N change on a running PLL, and it is not
244144 either. Measuring it needs the `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-cpu-pll-ccu` and `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu` CPU-PLL driver on a board,
and has not been done.

What is left is exact rather than invented: with the ramp delay set,
`dev_pm_opp_get_max_transition_latency()` = volt latency + clock latency
(opp/core.c:396) = 250 mV / 1280 uV/us = 196 us + 0. So the number the
governor sees is the voltage component only, every nanosecond of it
traceable to a register we read, and it is a lower bound on the true
transition cost -- same order as the vendor's 244 us, so no governor
behaves differently. When the CPU-PLL driver can be asked for a real
relock time, `clock-latency-ns` is one line and it is then a measurement.

THE DSU SHARES DCDC5
====================

DCDC5 is one rail feeding VDD-CPUL and VDD-DSU together, so every A55
voltage request is also the DSU's voltage. The DSU is not scaled by
anything in this tree and has no DT representation at all, which makes
this the one constraint nothing else in the kernel can enforce.

It runs at 780 MHz, and that is register-read, not assumed:

  CPU_DSU_PLL_CTRL @0x08873000 = 0xF8801E00
    -> N = 0x1E = 30, P = 0+1, M0 = 0+1  ->  26 MHz * 30 = 780.000 MHz
  CPU_DSU_PLL_CLK  @0x0887301c = 0x03000105
    -> mux bits[26:24] = 0b011 = own PLL / P, EXT_DIVP = 00 = /1
    -> DSU core 780 MHz, AXI/GIC/APB dividers /2 = 390 MHz

Two witnesses, byte-identical: the 6 GiB board read on 2026-07-22 for this patch,
the 8 GiB board in a readout on the vendor image. Nothing in our tree writes 0x08873000 -- `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu`
exposes CLK_CPU_L and CLK_CPU_B, not the DSU PLL -- so it stays where
boot0 put it for the life of the boot.

Why 800 mV is enough at 780 MHz: because both boards are doing it right
now. DCDC5 measures 800 mV (0x16 = 0x9e) while the DSU PLL measures
780 MHz, on both chips. That is the operating point itself, not a model
of it. Corroboration only, not evidence: the vendor's own DSU vf table
lists 780 MHz at 800 mV in all nine bins.

Both layers of this patch respect it. The lowest voltage in every A55
column -- speed4, speed5 and worst case alike -- is 800 mV, and
`regulator-min-microvolt = <800000>` makes anything lower fail with
-EINVAL in the regulator core rather than reach the rail. A future patch
that scales the DSU has to raise this floor to the DSU's requirement at
its highest frequency; it cannot simply add an OPP.

cpu-supply PLACEMENT
====================

On cpu@0 and cpu@600 only. cpufreq-dt calls `dt_cpufreq_early_init()` per
CPU ascending (cpufreq-dt.c:287) and every CPU already covered by a
policy returns immediately via `cpufreq_dt_find_data()`, so
`find_supply_name()` (cpufreq-dt.c:63) only ever looks at cpu0 and cpu6.
The regulator then belongs to the shared OPP table, not to the cpu node
(`_opp_set_regulators()`, opp/core.c:2241).

What groups the CPUs is `opp-shared`, not `cpu-map`:
`dev_pm_opp_of_get_sharing_cpus()` (opp/of.c) puts every CPU whose
`operating-points-v2` phandle is the same node into one policy. The
`cpu-map` from patch `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-topology` feeds the scheduler's topology and has no say
here. The same function is why all EIGHT cpu nodes get
`operating-points-v2`: it walks every possible CPU and returns -ENOENT if
one of them has no OPP node.

The properties are attached through label references at the end of the
file rather than edited into the cpu@N nodes, which `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu` has just changed
in place to add the CPU-PLL mux clocks. Two patches inserting a few lines
apart into one node is how a series stops applying. This is also the
shape a board file uses when the cpu nodes live in a .dtsi.

PRIOR ART
=========

An OPP-v2 patch in this project's earlier Armbian-based port of this board
did this first
and is where the worst-case column comes from: per-frequency cumulative
maximum of the 26 MHz vf columns, monotonic by construction. It is
running on a second 6 GiB board today. Its weakness is measured, not theoretical: it has
no bin filtering at all, so it ships the union of every bin's frequency
ladder. On that second 6 GiB board it was caught running 1300 MHz (CPU PLL N=50) at 800 mV, a step
its own bin 0x05 has no voltage entry for. In the vendor kernel that
filtering happens only as a side effect of
`_opp_supported_by_regulators()` rejecting the zero-voltage entries,
which needs `cpu-supply` wired first. This patch does it deliberately
instead, and ships only the steps its own bin characterised.

The other half of the price was efficiency: one table for all bins means
every chip pays the worst bin. Against that table, this one saves 190 mV
at 1612 MHz on a bin-0x05 A55 (860 vs 1050 mV, about a third of the
dynamic power on that rail at that step) and 50 mV at the A76 top step
(1000 vs 1050 mV, about 9 %).

NO EXTRA OPERATING POINTS
=========================

None are added, and that is a decision rather than an oversight. The
clock side would take them: N is a free 8-bit field, so 26 MHz steps
between 1014 and 2002 MHz are all reachable with the VCO inside its
480..2260 MHz window. The voltage side would not. The UM contains no
V/f curve, the datasheet contains only the 1.2 V rating, and a voltage
interpolated between two measured points is exactly the class of number
this repo does not put into a DT node. For bins 0x04 and 0x05 the vendor
characterised precisely the steps shipped here; there is nothing extra to
enable.

For the same reason no OPP is marked `opp-turbo`, so the top A76 step
needs no `boost` knob to be reachable.

`opp-microwatt` (Energy Model, EAS) is left out: those numbers have to be
measured on a rail, not derived, and that belongs with the thermal patches.

WHAT THIS PATCH DEPENDS ON, AND WHAT IS STILL UNPROVEN
======================================================

Depends on `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-topology` (cpu nodes + cpu-map), `arm64-dts-a733-enable-r-i2c0-axp318w-pmic` (AXP318W PMIC node), `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo` +
`arm64-dts-a733-model-the-26mhz-dcxo` (26 MHz DCXO), `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu` (CLK_CPU_L / CLK_CPU_B on the cpu nodes --
without a `clocks` property `cpufreq_init()` cannot get the CPU clock and
the policy never comes up) and `nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse` (speed bin driver, SID node,
`cpu_speed_grade` cell, binding compatible). DT-only.

Verified for this patch: applies to the tree with `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo`, `arm64-dts-a733-model-the-26mhz-dcxo`, `nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse` and `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu` in
place; dtc compiles the result with zero warnings; the resulting dtb was
decompiled and checked property by property -- both cpu-supply phandles
resolve to the intended regulator nodes, both `nvmem-cells` resolve to
`cpu-speed-grade@4c`, all eight CPUs carry the right table phandle, and
every voltage lands as intended (spot-checked at the A76 2002 MHz entry:
worst case and speed4 = 1050000, speed5 = 1000000).

The register reads quoted above are reads of the CURRENT state; they do
not prove that this description produces it.

The one observable that decides it, on the 6 GiB board (bin 0x04):

  boot with the series up to and including this patch, set the A55 policy to 1794000, then read, with
  the kernel out of the loop:
      i2cget -f -y <bus> 0x36 0x16   must be 0xb2 (DVM + n=50 = 1000 mV)
      CPU_L_PLL_CTRL @0x08871000 bits[15:8] must be 0x45 (N=69, 1794 MHz)
  Both at the same time. If 0x16 still reads 0x9e (800 mV) while the PLL
  shows N=69, the OPP voltage is not reaching the rail and this patch is
  wrong in the dangerous direction; if the PLL never leaves N=39, the
  table was never accepted.

Second acceptance criterion, and the reason for that check: the boot log
must contain no "vdd-cpub: Bringing ..." or "vdd-cpul-dsu: Bringing ..."
line, and neither rail may be logged as "disabling". Both rails sit at
800 mV, both windows start at 800 mV, so probe has nothing to do.

Third, cheap and worth doing in the same session: the log must say
"A733: speed bin 4 (fuse word 0x00000496)" on the 6 GiB board. If it says
"using the worst-case OPP column" instead, the bin path is broken and
every voltage above is the worst case rather than the measured one.

By consumer this is a CPU patch and would belong with the early CPU patches. It cannot
sit there. Hunk 1 adds the two CPU rails inside the `regulators` node that patch
`arm64-dts-a733-enable-r-i2c0-axp318w-pmic` creates, and `arm64-dts-a733-enable-r-i2c0-axp318w-pmic` applies later. Applying only the early CPU patches
shows no PMIC node at all, so this is a real dependency and not a diff artefact.

Splitting it was considered and rejected as unsafe. The half without cpu-supply
would leave a bootable intermediate state that offers every operating point up
to 2002 MHz while the rails still hold the 800 mV boot0 leaves behind -- an
undervolt on exactly the steps that need the most. One patch, ordered after its
dependency, has no such window.

