+/*
+ * CPU DVFS: OPP tables + cpu-supply.
+ *
+ * Attached by label instead of edited into the cpu@N nodes, which patch `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu`
+ * has just changed in place to add the CPU-PLL mux clocks. Two patches
+ * inserting a few lines apart into one node is how a series stops applying.
+ * This is also the shape a board file would use if the cpu nodes lived in a
+ * .dtsi, so nothing here is unusual to read.
+ *
+ * cpu-supply sits on cpu@0 and cpu@600 only, and that is not an omission:
+ * cpufreq-dt calls dt_cpufreq_early_init() per CPU ascending
+ * (cpufreq-dt.c:287) and every CPU already covered by a policy returns
+ * immediately via cpufreq_dt_find_data(), so find_supply_name()
+ * (cpufreq-dt.c:63) only ever looks at cpu0 and cpu6. opp-shared is what puts
+ * cpu1..cpu5 into cpu0's policy (dev_pm_opp_of_get_sharing_cpus(), opp/of.c)
+ * -- cpu-map does not, it feeds the scheduler topology. The regulator then
+ * belongs to the shared OPP table, not to the cpu node (_opp_set_regulators(),
+ * opp/core.c:2241).
+ *
+ * operating-points-v2 has to be on ALL eight CPUs even so:
+ * dev_pm_opp_of_get_sharing_cpus() iterates every possible CPU and bails out
+ * with -ENOENT ("Couldn't find opp node") if one of them lacks the phandle.
+ */
